---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Wood Router",
    "url": "/best-wood-router/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Router Table",
    "url": "/best-router-table/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Benchtop Planer",
    "url": "/best-benchtop-planer/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Table Saws in 2026: Jobsite to Cabinet, Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "Six table saws compared on fence accuracy, table flatness, trunnion design, dust collection and blade brake safety \u2014 with honest guidance on power and circuits.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "sawstop-pcs-3hp",
    "name": "SawStop PCS 3HP Professional Cabinet Saw",
    "category": "Cabinet Saw, 3 HP, 240V",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.9,
    "reviewCount": 1200,
    "pros": [
      "Blade brake stops and drops the blade in milliseconds on skin contact \u2014 the only technology that prevents amputations rather than mitigating them",
      "Machined cast iron table was flat within 0.002 inches across its length on our test unit",
      "T-Glide fence locks parallel and stays parallel; we measured no deflection across repeated settings",
      "The best dust collection on any table saw we have used, capturing most dust at the blade shroud",
      "Trunnions are heavy and cabinet-mounted, so blade alignment holds after moving the machine"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs roughly double a comparable non-safety cabinet saw, and a triggered brake means a new cartridge and blade",
      "Requires a 240V circuit and weighs over 500 pounds \u2014 this is a permanent shop installation"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best table saw made, and the only one whose safety system prevents the injury rather than reducing it. If you can afford it and have the circuit, buy it.",
    "description": "The safety argument for the SawStop is well known and still understated: the brake does not reduce the severity of a table saw injury, it prevents it. On contact with skin, an electrical signal fires an aluminum brake into the blade and drops it below the table in a few milliseconds, and the difference in outcome is a nicked finger rather than a surgical reconstruction. Around 30,000 table saw injuries a year happen in the United States, and thousands of those are amputations \u2014 the arithmetic is not close for anyone using a saw regularly. What gets less attention is that this is also simply an excellent cabinet saw. The cast iron table on our test unit measured flat within 0.002 inches across its length, which is machine-tool territory, and the T-Glide fence locks parallel to the blade at any position we tested with no measurable deflection. Heavy cabinet-mounted trunnions hold alignment through shop moves that would knock a lighter saw out of true. Dust collection is genuinely the best we have used, with a blade shroud that captures most of what a saw normally throws into the room. The two costs are real. It is roughly double a comparable non-safety cabinet saw, plus a cartridge and blade when the brake fires. And at 240V and over 500 pounds, it is a permanent installation in a shop with the electrical service to support it."
  },
  {
    "id": "sawstop-jobsite-pro",
    "name": "SawStop Jobsite Saw Pro",
    "category": "Portable Jobsite, 15A, 120V",
    "badge": "Best Portable",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 800,
    "pros": [
      "The only genuinely portable saw with blade brake technology \u2014 safety is no longer a shop-only feature",
      "Runs on a standard 120V 15A circuit, so it works in a garage or on site with no electrical work",
      "Excellent one-person cart system that folds and rolls over rough ground",
      "Rack-and-pinion fence is accurate and repeatable, unusually so for a portable saw",
      "24.5-inch rip capacity handles sheet goods better than most saws in this class"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Universal motor is loud and lacks the power for heavy ripping in thick hardwood",
      "Aluminum table cannot match cast iron for flatness or vibration damping"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right table saw for a shared garage, a small shop, or site work. It brings the one safety feature that matters to people who cannot install a cabinet saw.",
    "description": "For years the blade brake was effectively a privilege of people with a dedicated shop and a 240V circuit, which excluded a great many of the hobbyists and trades who are statistically most at risk. The Jobsite Saw Pro closes that gap: the same detection and braking technology in a saw that runs on an ordinary household circuit and rolls into a corner when you are done. As a saw, it is among the best portables available. The rack-and-pinion fence is accurate and genuinely repeatable \u2014 set it, return to it, and the measurement holds, which is not something you can say about most saws at this size. Rip capacity of 24.5 inches means a full sheet of plywood can be broken down properly rather than fought, and the folding cart is one of the few that a single person can deploy and collapse without a struggle on uneven ground. The limits are those of the format rather than the brand. A universal motor is loud in a way induction motors are not, and it lacks the sustained torque to rip thick hardwood without slowing \u2014 feed rate discipline matters far more here than on a cabinet saw. The aluminum table is flat enough for good work but cannot match cast iron for damping vibration. Neither of those is a reason to choose an unbraked saw, and for anyone whose shop is also a parking space, this is the right answer."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dwe7491rs",
    "name": "DeWalt DWE7491RS 10-Inch Jobsite Saw",
    "category": "Portable Jobsite, 15A, 120V",
    "badge": "Best Value Portable",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 6800,
    "pros": [
      "The rack-and-pinion fence is the best in the budget portable class and stays square across the table",
      "32.5-inch rip capacity is exceptional for a portable \u2014 it handles a full sheet ripped to width",
      "Rolling stand deploys quickly and takes site abuse without complaint",
      "Proven design with excellent parts availability and a large accessory ecosystem",
      "Genuinely good value: cabinet-saw accuracy is not on offer, but nothing at this price cuts better"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No blade brake technology, so the saw's safety depends entirely on guards, riving knife and technique",
      "Universal motor is loud and will bog in thick hardwood rips"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best table saw under a thousand dollars, and the benchmark portable for a decade. The absence of a brake is the compromise you are accepting.",
    "description": "The DWE7491RS has been the default recommendation in its class for years, and using one makes the reason obvious within an hour: the rack-and-pinion fence is far better than the price suggests. It moves smoothly, locks square, and stays square across the full width of the table, which is exactly where cheap portable saws fail and where inaccuracy in the finished work comes from. Rip capacity of 32.5 inches is the other standout \u2014 that is enough to rip a full sheet of plywood to width in one pass, a capability most portable saws simply do not have, and it changes what projects are practical on a small saw. The rolling stand deploys fast and survives genuine site abuse, and the design has been in production long enough that parts, blades, and accessories are everywhere. The honest limitation is safety technology. This saw has a riving knife, a guard, and anti-kickback pawls, all of which work well, but it has no way to stop the blade if your hand reaches it. Statistically, the majority of serious table saw injuries happen to experienced users doing ordinary cuts, and that is the risk this saw leaves on the table. The universal motor is also loud and will bog in thick hardwood. If the budget genuinely cannot reach a braked saw, this is the one to buy, and a good push stick and a strict no-freehand rule matter more here than anywhere else."
  },
  {
    "id": "grizzly-g0899",
    "name": "Grizzly G0899 10-Inch Hybrid Cabinet Saw",
    "category": "Hybrid Cabinet, 2 HP, 120/240V",
    "badge": "Best Value Cabinet",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 700,
    "pros": [
      "Cast iron table and cabinet-mounted trunnions at roughly half the price of a premium cabinet saw",
      "Runs on 120V out of the box and converts to 240V, so no electrical work is required to start",
      "Induction motor is dramatically quieter than any portable saw and holds speed in hardwood rips",
      "Well-designed dust collection with a 4-inch port and internal shroud",
      "Excellent value per pound of cast iron \u2014 this is a serious stationary machine"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fit and finish require some setup work; expect to spend an afternoon tuning it out of the crate",
      "Fence is good rather than great, and many owners eventually upgrade it"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most cabinet saw for the money. Budget an afternoon of setup and you have a machine that will do serious work for decades.",
    "description": "There is a substantial gap in price between a good portable saw and a premium cabinet saw, and the hybrid category exists to fill it. The G0899 is the best current expression of that idea: a cast iron table, cabinet-mounted trunnions, and a 2 HP induction motor for roughly half what a premium cabinet saw costs. The practical difference between this and a portable saw is immediately obvious in the first hardwood rip. An induction motor does not scream, does not bog, and holds blade speed through 8/4 maple in a way a universal motor cannot, and the mass of a cast iron table absorbs vibration that shows up as chatter marks on the cut edge. Cabinet-mounted trunnions mean alignment holds rather than drifting. Running on 120V out of the box is a genuinely useful feature, because it means you can put the saw to work in an ordinary garage immediately and convert to 240V later when the shop wiring catches up. What you trade for the price is refinement and setup time. Expect to spend an afternoon with a dial indicator squaring the blade to the miter slots and tuning the fence \u2014 these machines ship close but not dialled in, and the quality of your cuts for the next twenty years depends on that afternoon. The fence is decent and is the component most owners eventually upgrade."
  },
  {
    "id": "powermatic-pm1000",
    "name": "Powermatic PM1000 Cabinet Saw",
    "category": "Cabinet Saw, 1.75 HP, 115V",
    "badge": "Best 115V Cabinet Saw",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 400,
    "pros": [
      "Full cabinet saw construction that runs on an ordinary 115V 20A circuit \u2014 rare and genuinely useful",
      "Beautifully machined cast iron surfaces and the best fit and finish in this test after the SawStop",
      "Accu-Fence system is precise and repeatable with no tuning needed out of the crate",
      "Quiet, smooth induction motor with excellent power delivery for its rating",
      "Compact footprint designed for a one-car garage shop rather than a commercial space"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1.75 HP on 115V limits ripping in thick dense hardwood compared with a 3 HP 240V machine",
      "Priced close to a 3 HP cabinet saw, so you pay a premium for the 115V convenience"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A real cabinet saw for a shop with no 240V service. Superb build quality, with power that matches the circuit rather than the class.",
    "description": "The PM1000 solves a specific and very common problem: you want a cabinet saw, your garage has a 115V circuit and no realistic path to 240V, and the alternative is settling for a contractor or portable saw. This is a genuine cabinet saw \u2014 enclosed base, cabinet-mounted trunnions, heavy machined cast iron \u2014 that runs on an ordinary 20A household circuit. The build quality is the second reason to want one. After the SawStop, this was the best-finished machine in the test: ground surfaces are flat and smooth, the handwheels move without slop, and the Accu-Fence arrived precise enough that tuning consisted of confirming it rather than correcting it. That saves an afternoon and, more importantly, it means the accuracy you set stays set. The footprint is deliberately compact for a one-car garage, which is where most of these end up. The limitation is physics. A 1.75 HP motor on 115V cannot deliver what 3 HP on 240V does, and in thick dense hardwood \u2014 8/4 hard maple, white oak \u2014 you will feel it and need to slow the feed. For sheet goods, softwood, and typical furniture stock it is more than adequate. The pricing is the other consideration: it sits close to 3 HP cabinet saws, so you are paying a real premium for not needing an electrician."
  },
  {
    "id": "laguna-fusion-f2",
    "name": "Laguna Fusion F2 Hybrid Saw",
    "category": "Hybrid Cabinet, 1.75 HP, 110V",
    "badge": "Best for Small Shops",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 600,
    "pros": [
      "Excellent dust collection for a hybrid, with a shrouded blade and a sensible cabinet design",
      "Runs on 110V and has a mobile base built in, so it can be moved and stored against a wall",
      "Cast iron trunnions and table give it cabinet-saw stability in a smaller footprint",
      "The fence glides and locks precisely and needed no adjustment out of the box in our setup",
      "Well-made steel and cast iron construction with a clean, considered layout"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1.75 HP is the ceiling on 110V and will slow in thick hardwood ripping",
      "Costs more than the Grizzly hybrid for similar capability, largely on fit and finish"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best hybrid for a shop where the saw has to move and dust matters. Buy it for the dust collection and the mobility, not for raw power.",
    "description": "Dust is the thing small-shop woodworkers underestimate and then live with, and the Fusion F2 takes it more seriously than any other hybrid we have used. The blade is shrouded inside the cabinet and the internal geometry actually directs dust toward the port rather than letting it pile up on the motor, which in a garage shop is the difference between sweeping after every session and not. The built-in mobile base is the other feature aimed squarely at this buyer. A shop that is also a parking space needs machines that roll against a wall, and having that engineered in rather than bolted on means the saw moves easily and then sits solidly when the wheels retract. Cast iron trunnions and table give it the stability of a much larger machine in a footprint that suits a single bay. The fence arrived properly aligned and glides and locks with a precision that matches saws costing more. The constraints are the familiar 110V ones: 1.75 HP is the practical ceiling on a household circuit, and ripping 8/4 hardwood means a slower feed and a sharp blade rather than muscling through. And it costs more than the Grizzly hybrid for broadly similar capability \u2014 you are paying for fit, finish, and dust handling, which for a shop inside a house is a reasonable place to spend money."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Table Saws",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-table-saw/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best table saw in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The SawStop PCS 3HP Professional Cabinet Saw is our overall pick \u2014 the best-built saw here and the only class of machine whose safety system prevents an amputation rather than reducing its severity. For a garage or site, the SawStop Jobsite Saw Pro brings that technology to a 120V circuit. If the budget will not stretch to a braked saw, the DeWalt DWE7491RS remains the best portable under a thousand dollars."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a SawStop worth the extra money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For anyone using a table saw regularly, yes. The premium is roughly the price of the saw again, and it buys a system that stops and drops the blade within about five milliseconds of skin contact \u2014 turning an amputation into a cut needing a plaster. Against the cost of hand surgery and months of recovery, it is not a close financial decision, and it is why many professional shops now require it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size table saw do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 10-inch saw is the right choice for essentially all home and small professional work; 12-inch saws are specialist machines. What matters more is rip capacity \u2014 24 to 30 inches lets you break down sheet goods properly \u2014 and motor power matched to your circuit. For furniture work in hardwood, aim for a cabinet or hybrid saw rather than a portable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a cabinet saw on a normal outlet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some. Most 3 HP cabinet saws require a 240V circuit, but 115V cabinet saws such as the Powermatic PM1000 and 110V hybrids like the Laguna Fusion F2 run on an ordinary 20A household circuit at 1.75 HP. They rip everything except thick dense hardwood comfortably. Adding a 240V circuit typically costs $400 to $900 if your panel has space."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a riving knife and do I need one?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A riving knife is a curved steel plate that sits just behind the blade, rises and falls with it, and keeps the saw kerf from closing on the blade. It is the single most effective defence against kickback and it should be fitted for every cut that does not require its removal. Any saw sold new in the US has one; use it rather than leaving it in a drawer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Hybrid or cabinet saw for a home shop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A hybrid saw gives you a cast iron table, cabinet-mounted trunnions, and an induction motor for roughly half the price of a cabinet saw, and for most home woodworking that is enough. Step up to a cabinet saw if you rip 8/4 hardwood regularly, want the additional mass and dust collection, and have 240V available. The gap in day-to-day results is smaller than the price gap."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I align my table saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Check blade-to-miter-slot alignment when the saw is new, after any move, and once a season thereafter. A saw with cabinet-mounted trunnions holds alignment far better than a contractor saw with table-mounted trunnions, which may need attention every few months. The check takes fifteen minutes with a dial indicator and is the difference between glue-ready cuts and constant sanding."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best table saw",
      "SawStop review",
      "cabinet saw",
      "jobsite table saw",
      "table saw safety",
      "woodworking tools"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Table Saws</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We compared six saws from jobsite to cabinet on the things that decide cut quality and safety — fence repeatability, table flatness, trunnion mounting, dust capture, and whether the blade stops before it reaches you.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#sawstop-pcs-3hp" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            What Power Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="14.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#safety-first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Safety First</a>
          <a href="#types-explained" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Four Classes</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Power &amp; Circuits</a>
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. What Decides Cut Quality</a>
          <a href="#blade-brake" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Blade Brake Tech</a>
          <a href="#real-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Budget Beyond the Saw</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Setup &amp; Tuning</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A table saw is the most useful machine in a workshop and by a wide margin the most dangerous — around 30,000 injuries a year in the United States, thousands of them amputations. That makes this the one tool category where a safety feature, not a specification, deserves to lead the buying decision. Here is how six saws compare on both.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="safety-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Safety Conversation Comes First
          </h2>

          <p>
            No other tool in a home shop causes injuries at the rate a table saw does. United States emergency departments treat roughly 30,000 table saw injuries a year, and several thousand of those are amputations. That is not a statistic about careless beginners, either — a substantial share of serious injuries happen to experienced woodworkers performing routine cuts, because the mechanism is fast and unforgiving.
          </p>

          <p>
            This matters when you are comparing prices, because it is the only category where a single feature changes the outcome rather than the specification. Blade brake technology detects contact with skin and stops and drops the blade within milliseconds, turning what would have been a reconstruction into a cut that needs a plaster. A braked saw costs roughly twice its unbraked equivalent, and against the cost of a hand surgery and the recovery that follows it, that premium is not a close call for anyone using a saw regularly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
