        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Four Things That Decide Cut Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>The fence.</strong> More than any other component, the fence determines the accuracy of your work. It must lock parallel to the blade at every position, return to the same measurement repeatedly, and not deflect when you press stock against it. A great fence on a mediocre saw produces better work than a mediocre fence on a great saw, which is why fence upgrades are the most common modification in woodworking.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Table flatness.</strong> Cast iron is flatter than aluminum, holds that flatness, and damps vibration that shows up as chatter on the cut edge. Premium saws are flat within a few thousandths of an inch across the table; budget portables can be ten times that, and it shows in glue-line quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Trunnion mounting and mass.</strong> Cabinet-mounted trunnions hold blade-to-slot alignment through moves and bumps; table-mounted ones drift. Heavy trunnions also damp vibration. This is the invisible component that separates saws that stay tuned from saws you re-square every few months.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dust collection.</strong> Not a convenience. Fine wood dust is a recognised respiratory hazard, and a saw with an enclosed cabinet, a blade shroud, and a properly sized port captures most of it at the source. An open contractor saw throws dust into the room no matter what you connect to it, and in a shop inside a house that matters a great deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="blade-brake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Blade Brake Technology, Honestly Assessed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The system works by running a small electrical signal through the blade and monitoring it for the change in capacitance that contact with a human body produces. On detection, a spring drives an aluminum block into the teeth and the blade's own momentum drives the assembly below the table. The whole event takes about five milliseconds — a hot dog test leaves a shallow nick, and real-world incident reports consistently describe minor cuts where an unbraked saw would have taken fingers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The costs are worth stating plainly. A brake activation destroys the cartridge, which is a consumable of roughly $70 to $100, and usually the blade with it, so a genuine save costs a few hundred dollars in parts. Wet or green wood, and metal contact, can also trigger it — there is a bypass mode for deliberately cutting conductive material, and forgetting to use it is how most false activations happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth being clear about what a brake does not do. It offers no protection against kickback, which is the other major table saw injury mechanism and one that puts people in hospital with broken ribs and worse. Kickback is prevented by the riving knife, correct fence alignment, never trapping stock between blade and fence, and standing out of the line of fire. A braked saw used carelessly is still dangerous; it simply removes the worst outcome from one of the two mechanisms.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a professional shop, insurers increasingly look favourably on braked saws, and in an environment where employees use the machine the case is effectively settled. For a home shop, the fair framing is this: it is the most expensive feature on the saw and the only one you would trade everything else for on the day it matters.
          </p>
        </div>

