        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Setup and Tuning Determine Everything After
          </h2>

          <p>
            A new saw is close to aligned, not aligned. Spending an afternoon with a dial indicator when the machine arrives is the highest-value time you will ever put into it, because every cut for the next twenty years inherits that setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three alignments matter, in order. First, the blade must be parallel to the miter slots — measure at the front and back of the blade with a dial indicator riding in the slot, and adjust the trunnions until the readings match within a few thousandths. Second, the fence must be parallel to the miter slots, or very slightly open at the rear, never toed in toward the blade, which causes binding and kickback. Third, the blade must be square to the table at the 90-degree stop, checked with a reliable square rather than the saw's own scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ongoing maintenance is light. Wax the cast iron with a paste wax containing no silicone, which cuts feed resistance noticeably and prevents surface rust. Keep the inside of the cabinet clear of packed dust, which affects both the motor's cooling and, on a braked saw, the brake mechanism. Check the alignment once a season or after moving the machine, and replace the blade well before it feels dull — a dull blade increases feed force, burns the cut, and makes kickback more likely.
          </p>
        </div>


