            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best table saw in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The SawStop PCS 3HP Professional Cabinet Saw is our overall pick — the best-built saw here and the only class of machine whose safety system prevents an amputation rather than reducing its severity. For a garage or site, the SawStop Jobsite Saw Pro brings that technology to a 120V circuit. If the budget will not stretch to a braked saw, the DeWalt DWE7491RS remains the best portable under a thousand dollars.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a SawStop worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anyone using a table saw regularly, yes. The premium is roughly the price of the saw again, and it buys a system that stops and drops the blade within about five milliseconds of skin contact — turning an amputation into a cut needing a plaster. Against the cost of hand surgery and months of recovery, it is not a close financial decision, and it is why many professional shops now require it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size table saw do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 10-inch saw is the right choice for essentially all home and small professional work; 12-inch saws are specialist machines. What matters more is rip capacity — 24 to 30 inches lets you break down sheet goods properly — and motor power matched to your circuit. For furniture work in hardwood, aim for a cabinet or hybrid saw rather than a portable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a cabinet saw on a normal outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some. Most 3 HP cabinet saws require a 240V circuit, but 115V cabinet saws such as the Powermatic PM1000 and 110V hybrids like the Laguna Fusion F2 run on an ordinary 20A household circuit at 1.75 HP. They rip everything except thick dense hardwood comfortably. Adding a 240V circuit typically costs $400 to $900 if your panel has space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a riving knife and do I need one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A riving knife is a curved steel plate that sits just behind the blade, rises and falls with it, and keeps the saw kerf from closing on the blade. It is the single most effective defence against kickback and it should be fitted for every cut that does not require its removal. Any saw sold new in the US has one; use it rather than leaving it in a drawer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Hybrid or cabinet saw for a home shop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A hybrid saw gives you a cast iron table, cabinet-mounted trunnions, and an induction motor for roughly half the price of a cabinet saw, and for most home woodworking that is enough. Step up to a cabinet saw if you rip 8/4 hardwood regularly, want the additional mass and dust collection, and have 240V available. The gap in day-to-day results is smaller than the price gap.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I align my table saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check blade-to-miter-slot alignment when the saw is new, after any move, and once a season thereafter. A saw with cabinet-mounted trunnions holds alignment far better than a contractor saw with table-mounted trunnions, which may need attention every few months. The check takes fifteen minutes with a dial indicator and is the difference between glue-ready cuts and constant sanding.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Table Saw Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A table saw is a twenty-year machine, and the errors people make choosing one tend to be errors they live with for the whole time.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Saw and a Cheap Blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The blade does the cutting. A good 40-tooth combination blade makes a mid-range saw cut beautifully, and a cheap blade makes a premium saw look mediocre. If the budget is spent after buying the machine, buy the blade before any other accessory — it is the cheapest meaningful upgrade in the shop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Fence in the Comparison</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fence quality decides accuracy more than any other component, and it is the one most buyers skim over while comparing motor ratings. Check that the fence locks parallel at every position and does not deflect under hand pressure. A saw with a poor fence will frustrate you for as long as you own it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the Riving Knife and Leaving It Off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It gets taken off for a non-through cut and never goes back, and that is when kickback happens. The riving knife is the most effective anti-kickback device on the machine, it does not interfere with normal cuts, and a saw with quick-release knife mounting has no excuse. Put it back before the next through cut.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Tuning a New Saw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Machines ship close to aligned, not aligned. An afternoon with a dial indicator squaring the blade to the miter slots and setting the fence pays back in every cut for the next two decades. Skipping it means chasing inaccuracies you will blame on technique.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating Dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fine wood dust is a recognised respiratory hazard, and a table saw is the largest producer of it in a small shop. Budget for collection at the same time as the saw — an enclosed cabinet with a shroud and a properly sized collector removes most of it at the source, while an open saw and a broom does not.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
